.TH CCIRCQ_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccircq_destroy \- destroy a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccircq_destroy(cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees everything the queue holds and then sets the queue pointer to
NULL.

ceventloop_remove() doesn't wait for a callback that it has
already handed to a reactor thread, so such a callback can still be
working inside this queue when the removal returns. This call blocks
until every one of those callbacks is done with the queue and only then
frees it, which is what makes the documented teardown order (drain,
remove, destroy) safe as written.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run that
whole sequence for this queue, with any num_reactor_threads. A dispatch
that the loop collected for the removed registration but did not start
yet doesn't hold the destroy up, and it never runs its callback after
the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

cq is evaluated exactly once and must be a modifiable lvalue, such as
a variable or an array element. An expression with a side effect, such
as handles[i++], therefore destroys and clears a single element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A message left in the queue causes an assertion failure, so drain the
queue before you destroy it. The messages are counted only after every
callback that the removal left running has finished, and a write
callback that was still running when ceventloop_remove() returned
can send, so its message fails the assertion too. When a write
registration watches the queue, remove it, wait for its on_removed
callback, and only then drain and destroy the queue.

A watcher on this queue causes an assertion failure as well. A watcher
is a cselect() call, a cselect_timed() call or a
ceventloop registration (ccircq_selectable or
cchan_selectable). Without the assertion the waiter would stay
linked and keep pointing at the mutex of this queue, which the destroy
is about to free. Call ceventloop_remove() first, or let every
cselect() and cselect_timed() call that watches the queue
return.

Do not call this from inside a ceventloop callback that dispatches
this queue, because the destroy would wait for that very callback. It
is a caller error, and the program stops through ccol__fatal_err().

Do not hold an application lock across this call when one of those
callbacks takes the same lock: The destroy waits for the callback while
the callback waits for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccircq_create (3)
